/hr4669/BILLS-119hr4669ih.htm"},"other":[{"label":"This bill on Congress.gov","url":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/4669"}],"past":[]},"sponsor":{"chamber":"House","district":"6","id":"G000546","name":"Rep. Graves, Sam","party":"R","state":"MO"},"stage":"reported","stage_label":"Reported by committee","summary":"The FEMA Act of 2025 reestablishes the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) as an independent, cabinet-level agency separate from the Department of Homeland Security. It enacts extensive reforms to FEMA's public assistance, individual assistance, and hazard mitigation programs, aiming to speed up disaster funding, reduce paperwork, and improve transparency.","summary_detail":{"affected_groups":[{"effect":"Gain access to new block grants for small disasters, streamlined project cost estimations, preapproved mitigation planning, and options to assume certain federal environmental review responsibilities.","group":"State, local, and tribal governments"},{"effect":"Experience a unified disaster application process, expanded eligibility for housing and hazard mitigation assistance, and improved notices and appeal rights regarding denied assistance.","group":"Disaster survivors and individuals"},{"effect":"Are eligible for expedited grants to repair or replace facilities providing critical services, subject to specific loan application and service criteria.","group":"Private nonprofit facility owners"},{"effect":"Undergo an administrative transition out of the Department of Homeland Security into an independent cabinet-level agency, alongside new staffing and operational requirements.","group":"FEMA and federal personnel"}],"key_provisions":["Reestablishes FEMA as an independent, cabinet-level agency in the executive branch headed by an Administrator who reports directly to the President.","Establishes new public assistance procedures, including expedited grants based on professional cost estimates for repairing or replacing public and critical non-profit facilities.","Creates a block grant option for small disasters, allowing state and tribal governments to receive a lump sum payment in lieu of traditional Public Assistance.","Establishes a unified disaster application system and universal application to streamline individual assistance and interagency information sharing.","Reforms pre-disaster mitigation programs by shifting to noncompetitive formula grants, establishing preapproved project plans, and setting up a peer review panel for projects.","Requires federal entities to publish online dashboards and conduct various studies and reviews related to disaster assistance, backlogs, fraud, and workforce retention."],"missing_information":["Exact overall appropriations levels or specific dollar authorizations for new grant programs are not fully detailed, as the text notes no additional funds are authorized for certain amendments or specifies reliance on future appropriations."],"plain_summary":"The FEMA Act of 2025 reestablishes the Federal Emergency Management Agency (FEMA) as an independent, cabinet-level agency separate from the Department of Homeland Security. It enacts extensive reforms to FEMA's public assistance, individual assistance, and hazard mitigation programs, aiming to speed up disaster funding, reduce paperwork, and improve transparency.","significance":"The legislation represents a sweeping reorganization of federal emergency management by elevating FEMA to an independent cabinet-level agency and fundamentally altering how disaster recovery, public infrastructure grants, individual aid, and hazard mitigation funds are administered and reviewed."},"summary_meta":{"generated_at":"2026-10-25T21:16:12+00:00","model":"gemini-3.5-flash-lite","source_basis":"text+summary","source_urls":["https://www.congress.gov/119/bills/hr4669/BILLS-119hr4669ih.htm"]},"summary_threshold":50,"title":"FEMA Act of 2025","viability":62.8,"viability_detail":{"breakdown":[{"available":true,"effective_we